  "step_3": "The decision reason focuses on an aspiration level (-2333.33) and evaluates which lottery has a higher chance of yielding an outcome above this level. In this case, only the 0 Euro outcome in Lottery A exceeds the aspiration level (by +2333.33), which occurs with 20% probability. Lottery B's outcome (-3000) is below the aspiration level, so its probability of a positive difference is 0%. Thus, the reason prefers A due to its 20% chance of a positive deviation. The individual also focuses on the 20% chance of a significantly better outcome (0 Euros vs losses), and this chance is central to their decision. Although the individual does not explicitly compute an aspiration level or refer to deviations from it, they are effectively responding to the same informational structure: the possibility of an outcome that is substantially less negative (i.e., 'better than expected' in a qualitative sense). The 20% chance of a 'good' outcome (0) is weighed against a higher loss, which aligns with the spirit of the aspiration-based reason. While the verbal report does not mention averaging outcomes or deviations from a mean, the cognitive focus on a threshold-like improvement (avoiding loss entirely) and the probabilistic trade-off mirrors the mechanism of the aspiration reason. Therefore, it is plausible that the individual is using a heuristic similar to the aspiration reason, even if not explicitly.",
