  "step_2": "The individual reports comparing probabilities and losses. They note that a loss is very likely in Lottery B. They reflect on whether risking a higher loss is worth it. They observe that the probability of no loss (i.e., 0 Euros) is over 50% in A (actually 10% — this seems to be a mistake in their reasoning) and only 10% in B (also incorrect — B has 55% chance of 0). Despite inaccuracies, the gist is that they focus on the chance of avoiding a loss and prefer A because it offers a better chance of no loss. However, their stated probability for A being 'over 50%' when it is actually 10% suggests confusion or misstatement. Still, they appear to base their decision on the probability of avoiding a loss (i.e., getting 0), favoring the option with higher probability of zero loss — which is B (55%) not A (10%). Their justification seems factually incorrect. Nevertheless, their reasoning centers on the likelihood of incurring no loss and weighs that against the magnitude of potential losses.",
  "step_3": "The decision reason relies on calculating an aspiration level (-3000) and then comparing how often each lottery yields outcomes above that level. Outcomes above -3000 are only the 0 Euro outcome in both lotteries. Lottery B provides this with 55% probability, A with 10%. The reason would prefer B. The individual, however, chooses A and justifies this by claiming A has a higher chance of no loss (over 50%) compared to B (10%), which is factually wrong — B has the higher chance of no loss. Their focus on the probability of no loss (0 Euros) aligns partially with the idea of outcomes above a reference point, but they do not mention any calculation of an aspiration level or comparison to -3000. They do not reference the mean of all outcomes or any such rule. Instead, their reasoning appears to be based on a misunderstanding of the probabilities and possibly a desire to avoid larger losses, but not through the specific rule of aspiration level based on mean outcomes. There is no evidence they computed the aspiration level or used differences from it. The similarity is superficial (focusing on 0 as a positive outcome), but the method and justification differ fundamentally. Therefore, it is unlikely the stated decision reason was used.",
  "final_assessment_score": 20
}

